sections.s2_technical:
The key distinction is single resource versus collection. When the URL identifies one specific resource, like `GET /accounts/SAV9999`, and that resource does not exist, the honest answer is 404 Not Found, because the client asked for a particular thing and it is not there. Returning 200 with an empty body here would be misleading, because the client cannot tell the difference between found-but-empty and not-found.

```java
// Single resource: 404 when absent.
@GetMapping("/accounts/{id}")
public ResponseEntity<AccountResponse> get(@PathVariable String id) {
    return service.find(id)
        .map(ResponseEntity::ok)
        .orElse(ResponseEntity.notFound().build()); // 404
}
```

When the URL identifies a collection, like `GET /accounts/SAV2001/transactions?minAmount=10000000`, and no transactions match, the collection itself still exists. The correct answer is 200 OK with an empty array `[]`. Zero results is a valid, successful answer to a valid query. Returning 404 here would be wrong, because it suggests the endpoint or account does not exist, confusing the client.

```java
// Collection: 200 with empty array when no matches.
@GetMapping("/accounts/{id}/transactions")
public ResponseEntity<List<TxnResponse>> list(@PathVariable String id,
        @RequestParam(required = false) BigDecimal minAmount) {
    List<TxnResponse> txns = service.findTxns(id, minAmount); // may be empty
    return ResponseEntity.ok(txns); // 200 even if txns is empty
}
```

204 No Content means the request succeeded and there is intentionally no body. It is the right choice after a successful DELETE or sometimes a PUT where you choose not to echo the resource. For a GET, 204 is almost never correct for the not-found case, because GET is a request for a representation, and no content muddles the meaning. A GET that found nothing single should say 404; a GET of a collection that found nothing should say 200 with an empty array.

There is a subtle collection case. If the parent in the path does not exist, such as `GET /accounts/SAV9999/transactions` where account SAV9999 itself does not exist, many teams return 404 because the parent resource is missing, while others return an empty 200. The cleaner approach is 404 when the parent path segment is genuinely invalid, and 200 with an empty array only when the parent exists but has no children.

A senior nuance: consistency across the API matters more than any single philosophical stance. Pick a rule — single missing is 404, collection empty is 200 with empty array — and apply it everywhere, document it, and make the client code simple. Clients handle empty arrays naturally by looping zero times, whereas a surprise 404 on an empty collection forces special-case error handling, which is a common source of bugs.

